      "boundElements": null,
      "updated": 1792473797194,
      "link": null,
      "locked": false,
      "text": "1. From JDK 5 onwards their came a special\ninterface called Queue in Collection Framework.\n\n2. It is child intereface of Collection interface .\n\n3. Since it is an interface we can not\ninstantiate it .\n\n4. The 3 most popular implementation class of\nQueue are\n\n-> LinkedList\n\n-> PriorotyQueue\n\n-> ArrayDeQueue\n\n5. Java's Queue doesn't gurantee to follow\nFIFO .\n\n6. It totally depend on Implementation class\nthat in which order it will process the data .\n\nfor ex- LinkedList follow FIFO order , \n\n-PriorityQueue process data based on priority .\n\n-ArrayDeQueue can process data either in LIFO\nor FIFI both .\n\n",
      "fontSize": 20,
      "fontFamily": 5,
      "textAlign": "left",
      "verticalAlign": "top",
      "containerId": null,
      "originalText": "1. From JDK 5 onwards their came a special interface called Queue in Collection Framework.\n\n2. It is child intereface of Collection interface .\n\n3. Since it is an interface we can not instantiate it .\n\n4. The 3 most popular implementation class of Queue are\n\n-> LinkedList\n\n-> PriorotyQueue\n\n-> ArrayDeQueue\n\n5. Java's Queue doesn't gurantee to follow FIFO .\n\n6. It totally depend on Implementation class that in which order it will process the data .\n\nfor ex- LinkedList follow FIFO order , \n\n-PriorityQueue process data based on priority .\n\n-ArrayDeQueue can process data either in LIFO or FIFI both .\n\n",
      "autoResize": false,
      "lineHeight": 1.25
    },
    {
